  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Я с тобой.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am with you.' The meaning hinges on 'I' working together with 'you' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Моя бабочка.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'My butterfly.' The meaning hinges on 'my' working together with 'butterfly' in spoken Russian. the doctor answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Воздух в себя больше.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Take more air in.' In Russian, the idea of 'air' flows naturally into 'more' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Давай, всё хорошо.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on, everything is fine.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'come on' and finishing on 'well'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Мы уже почти на месте.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We are almost there already.' Notice how Russian expresses 'we' and 'place' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Давай, дышим.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on, let's breathe.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'come on' before landing on 'we breathe'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Совесть есть?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do you have a conscience?' The grammar packs 'conscience' and 'there is' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ребёнку всего пять месяцев.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The baby is only five months along.' Vika anchors the line on 'to the baby', while 'months' completes the thought. the doctor answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "doctor",
   "t": "Что же вы, мамочка, а?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is with you, mommy, huh?' In Russian, the idea of 'what' flows naturally into 'and' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Совсем рассеянными стали.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We have become completely absent-minded.' The meaning hinges on 'at all' working together with 'steel' in spoken Russian.",
